// KOB router: KOB's composition layer, an Argent app on top of the
// hand-written orders. docs/argent/argent.md, "The router", specifies it.
//
// The router imports the published apps KOBOrders and KOBOrdersKron and
// observes KobAsk, KobBid and KobBidKron by closed ICC, by their published
// template hashes. It observes the token programs by open ICC. Each intent's
// state carries the handle of every token it moves, actor_type<KCC20State>
// or actor_type<KronTokenState>. The payer sets it, and a verifier such as
// the x402 facilitator recomputes it from its token allowlist. The router
// reads every observed token input and checks every pinned token output
// under that handle. So one actor serves every KCC-20 program of the 112-byte
// state and every KRON program of the 46-byte state. Each order validates
// itself, and the router validates what the payer cares about.
//
// Each intent is one covenant UTXO that any keeper or facilitator can spend.
// The payer signs nothing at execution time.
//   KasToToken      The payer locks KAS. The merchant receives exactly
//                   `amount` of token B, bought from one to three KobAsks.
//   TokenToKas      The payer locks token A. The merchant receives at least
//                   `merchant_kas`, raised by selling at most `max_sell` of A
//                   into one to three KobBids.
//   TokenSwap       The payer locks token A. The merchant receives exactly
//                   `amount_b` of token B. At most `max_sell_a` of A goes into
//                   one or two KobBids, and the KAS buys B from one or two
//                   KobAsks.
//   TokenToKasKron  The same as TokenToKas and TokenSwap, with a KRON token A
//   TokenSwapKron   sold into KobBidKrons. The merchant never receives a KRON
//                   token. The lock is id_type 2, and the deliveries and the
//                   payer's change are id_type 3. A KRON output holds
//                   1..=1e9 units, so the change cannot be zero, and a KRON
//                   intent locks more than it may sell.
// What an order releases beyond what the payer authorised, such as spread and
// tips, is the keeper's. The intent's parameters fix the payer's worst case.
//
// The payer may cancel at any time, signed with SIGHASH_ALL. A token
// intent's cancel must spend the lock at input j + 1, because tokens owned by
// the intent's id could never move after the covenant ends.
//
// FILL SHAPES. An `observes` group has an exact shape, and a covenant's
// redeem script carries every entry of its actor. So each fill shape is its
// own actor, with the shape's entry, expire and cancel. The payer picks the
// shape at creation, such as KasToToken_buy2_out or TokenSwap_swap. Per order
// there are two shapes.
//   rest  The order is filled partially and continues, GTC orders only. A
//         KobAsk continues with amountLeft - n and its custody holds the
//         rest. A KobBid continues byte-identically.
//   out   The order ends. A KobAsk is sold out, whatever its time in force. A
//         KobBid ends when exhausted, IOC or FOK.
// Only the last order of a sweep may rest, as a best-price-first matcher
// produces. The entry arguments list the orders in transaction order, and
// the asks' escrows are matched to them by owner. A leg has at most 3
// orders, so a token group has at most 3 escrow inputs and 4 token outputs.
// That fits the 8/8 prototype and KRON's 4/5. A 3/3 program runs every shape
